# XSH fread()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fread.001
anchor     functions/fread.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream reading the 13-byte file "0123456789abc": fread(b, 1, 4,
           f) returns 4 and stores "0123"; fread(b, 3, 2, f) then returns 2
           and stores "456789"; ftell() is then 10 and the next fgetc()
           returns 'a'.
test       t/001-elements.c

assertion  XSH.fread.002
anchor     functions/fread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       From a file holding the 8 bytes 0x00 0xff 0x80 0x0a 0x41 0x00 0x7f
           0xfe, fread(obj, 2, 4, f) into an array of four 2-byte objects
           returns 4, and the objects' bytes are those 8 bytes in file
           order.
note       Issue 6 added the rule that each object is filled, byte by byte
           in the order read, through an unsigned char array overlaying
           it.
test       t/002-bytes-in-order.c

assertion  XSH.fread.003
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       On a stream reading the 5-byte file "abcde", fread(b, 2, 4, f)
           returns 2 (whole elements only), the first 4 bytes stored are
           "abcd", feof() is non-zero, ferror() is 0, and ftell() is 5 (all
           5 bytes were read).
note       The value of the partly read third element is unspecified and
           is not looked at (variant fread.partial_junk).
test       t/003-short-at-eof.c

assertion  XSH.fread.004
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       On a stream from fdopen() of a pipe's read end, with the writer
           sending "abc", pausing 300 ms, then sending "defghij" and closing,
           fread(b, 1, 10, f) returns 10 and stores "abcdefghij" (a pipe
           that has only part of the data at first is neither an error nor
           end-of-file).
note       The count may be short only for an error or end-of-file.
           Austin Group defects 1196 and 1624 (Issue 8) reworded the
           RETURN VALUE section; the change history does not say which
           edition first stated this, so "since" is left out.
test       t/004-pipe-pieces.c

assertion  XSH.fread.005
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       With size 0 or nitems 0, fread() returns 0 and changes neither the
           array nor the stream: after reading "01" from "0123456789", both
           calls leave the array as it was, ftell() 2, feof() and ferror() 0,
           and the next fgetc() returns '2'; on a stream with the end-of-file
           indicator set it stays set, and on one with the error indicator
           set it stays set.
note       The change history does not say which edition added the rule
           (Austin Group defect 1624 reworded it in Issue 8), so "since" is
           left out. The error indicator is set by a read on a closed
           descriptor, which is then restored with dup2().
test       t/005-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fread.006
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
says       On a new stream reading "0123456789" whose descriptor has been
           closed with close(), fread(b, 1, 4, f) returns less than 4, sets
           the error indicator, and sets errno to EBADF.
note       The page refers to fgetc() for its errors; data needs to be read
           because nothing has been read yet. If the stream had already
           taken all 4 bytes in (nothing requires that, nothing forbids
           it), the test is UNRESOLVED. close() does not move the offset,
           so the descriptor is not a second handle (XSH 2.5.1). The
           change history does not say which edition made errno
           required, so "since" is left out.
test       t/006-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fread.007
anchor     functions/fread.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fgetc.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
says       On a stream from fdopen() of the read end of an empty pipe whose
           writer is still open, with O_NONBLOCK set, fread(b, 1, 4, f)
           returns 0, sets the error indicator, and sets errno to EAGAIN.
note       The change history does not say which edition made errno
           required, so "since" is left out.
test       t/007-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.fread.008
anchor     functions/fread.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       A first fread() that returns data from a file whose last data
           access time was set 100 s before its modification time marks the
           access time for update: fstat() afterwards shows it later than
           the value set.
note       The access time is set before the modification time, so a
           relatime mount updates it too; a file system mounted noatime
           fails this, as XBD 4.12 requires the update (as XSH.read.014).
           Issue 7 reworded the rule for fine-grained timestamps; the
           change history does not say when it was added, so "since" is
           left out.
test       t/008-atime.c
